# Kabyle pm surfaces.
# "n tmeddit" is CLDR 47's dayPeriods pm for kab (cldr-dates-full/main/kab/
# ca-gregorian.json) and is how kab.wikipedia writes a pm clock reading:
#   "Deg wass-nni, ɣef 4:40 n tmeddit, yiwet n terbaεt n yisafagen ..." (Bgayet)
#   "azal n 12:30 n tmeddit, takarust n Meɛṭub tettwaḥbes" (Lwennas Meɛṭub)
#   "ɣef lxemsa n tmeddit, anmahal n Ldzayer deg Fṛansa yemlal ..."
#     (Tafsut n Yimaziɣen)
n tmeddit
tmeddit
tameddit
# "uzal" is the broad-daylight noun; on the clock it closes a midday reading.
# The genitive "n uzal" is the standard form.  @athmanemokraoui, native Kabyle
# speaker, on
# https://github.com/TigreGotico/chronologia/pull/846#issuecomment-5573978767:
#   "'D ttnac n uzal' is the grammatically correct and standard form (genitive
#    n + annexed state uzal).  'D ttnac uzal' is only an informal elision in
#    rapid speech."
n uzal
# The bare apposition is what Tatoeba writes -- "Tella qrib d ttnac uzal."
# ("It was nearly noon.") -- and it stays readable: an informal register is
# still Kabyle a speaker says.  The form to write is "n uzal" above.
uzal
